WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:17.562
<v Jamie>Iran just torched a drone over the Strait of Hormuz, oil spiked, and Americans are looking at an extra hundred billion dollars in energy bills this year—right as the Fed's about to keep rates punishing.

00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:36.359
<v Alex>Markets are pretending nothing happened. S&P closed down zero point three eight percent, Nasdaq down zero point two nine. But the VIX just jumped five point three, which means volatility traders woke up and smelled the geopolitical coffee. Meanwhile, AMD is up four point six nine percent because apparently the only thing that sells chips faster than war is the fear of war.

00:00:36.359 --> 00:00:54.569
<v Jamie>So the big winners are all chip and cloud companies, and the losers are the rest of the world. Adobe down six point seven three, Tesla down five point nine two, Netflix down five point three five. It's like the market is sorting itself into two waiting rooms—one for companies that sell to the military-industrial complex and one for everything else.

00:00:54.569 --> 00:01:12.192
<v Maya>And Oracle's up three point zero eight percent because enterprise software that nobody actually wants to use becomes essential the second geopolitical risk rises. It's the opposite of a luxury good—it's a 'oh God, we need to secure our systems' good. Also, Apple's down two point five one, which means even the iPhone can't outrun a drone strike headline.

00:01:12.192 --> 00:01:23.647
<v Alex>August inflation data hits this week, and if it's hot, the Fed's entire 'patient' story falls apart. Rate cuts get pushed into the future, which means that hundred-billion-dollar energy bill lands on consumers while borrowing costs stay punishing.

00:01:23.647 --> 00:01:29.521
<v Jamie>So we're not just paying more for gas, we're also paying more to borrow money to pay for gas.

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:37.157
<v Alex>That's the shape of it, yes. The Fed's been hinting at relief since June. One hot print this week and that entire narrative gets torched.

00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:46.556
<v Maya>Nvidia posted earnings that were strong—like, genuinely solid results, reinforce the whole AI thesis—and the stock barely moved. The market's already priced in perfection, so even 'strong' is just 'not disappointing.'

00:01:46.556 --> 00:01:49.787
<v Jamie>So they crushed it and got punished for crushing it?

00:01:49.787 --> 00:01:58.011
<v Maya>Exactly. The bar's so high that excellence is the baseline. Miss it by a millimeter and the stock craters. Hit it dead-on and you get a shrug.

00:01:58.011 --> 00:02:06.529
<v Alex>A beverage brand just filed Chapter eleven after losing a Costco deal. One customer, gone, and the entire company implodes. That's what happens when your revenue is heavily concentrated.

00:02:06.529 --> 00:02:20.333
<v Jamie>Temu just became a car brand. There's a knockoff Range Rover circulating, and Jaguar Land Rover is watching its brand equity get fast-fashioned in real time. Legacy automakers are realizing that speed-to-market from a Chinese EV startup is a different animal than anything they've faced before.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5y4l22p262o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:27.676
<v Maya>Thousands of people showed up to the funeral of Ratko Mladić in Serbia. It's like watching a war crimes conviction get a standing ovation.

00:02:27.676 --> 00:02:33.550
<v Jamie>So the response is to pack a stadium and prove that infamy is just legacy with better attendance numbers?

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.837
<v Alex>So America just paid Iran a hundred billion dollars to make a point about drone flight paths.

00:02:38.837 --> 00:02:42.949
<v Maya>The most expensive way to say 'don't do that again' in recorded history.

00:02:42.949 --> 00:02:47.061
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
